        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The objectives of this research project are to optimize the sweetpotoato syrup, and to explore the economic value and marketebalility of sweetpotatoes and peanuts.  
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
The methodology for this experiment is not available.
<P>
RESULTS:<br>
<u>Sweetpotato syrup. </u><br>
Over the past year, an improved, acceptable color was accomplished, chemical analyses (% starch, Brix), consumer testing, descriptive analysis, and rheology studies were completed. Use of hydrochloric acid was removed from the protocol for the syrup. 
<p>
<u>Sweetpotato bread. </u><br>
Bread was designed using four levels of sweetpotato flour (SPF) and wheat flour (WWF). The appropriate formulations for the bread were developed in our Food Processing Laboratory. Chemical analyses, loaf volume, moisture content, color, and texture, Differential Scanning Calorimetry (DSC) X-ray Diffraction, Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM) and Nuclear Magnetic Resonance (NMR)and Stress Relaxation Tests were done on the different breads. Extruded ready-to-serve extruded breakfast cereal (RTSBC). Three different RTSBC with the following combinations were developed: 100% sweetpotato flour (SPF); 75/25%SPF/wheat bran (SPFWWB); and 100% wheat bran (WWB). Quantitative descriptive analysis and consumer testing were completed over the past year and a MS thesis was completed on the work. 
<p>
<u>Shelf stability of field-grown sweetpotatoes.</u><br>
In collaboration with the GEDI group, FOTU evaluated the proximate composition, color, texture and microbial characteristics of six sweet potato lines (J6-76, 94-95, WC-91, UNK3, TU-00-02 and Beauregard) during storage. The beginning and ending mean moisture contents were similar for all the sweetpotatoes except J6-76 and UNK3, which were significantly lower. Protein content and weight loss were significantly higher for UNK3 than the others. Beauregard had the deepest orange color, and UNK3 was the firmest sweetpotato. At the end of the study, UNK3 had the highest bacterial count. 
<end>
        </div>

